-- Passage rg8-connections-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('cf8e6bc1-de38-5922-9a3a-00d624025b0d', 'rg8-connections-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$The Canal and the Railroad$q$, $q$In 1830, the town of Harlow Falls was a quiet farming village of about four hundred people. Farmers who wanted to sell grain had to haul it forty miles by wagon over muddy roads. The trip took nearly a week, and much of the profit disappeared into the cost of feeding horses.

That changed in 1836, when workers finished the Harlow Canal. Flat boats pulled by mules could now carry grain to the city in three days. Because shipping became cheaper, farmers planted more wheat. Within ten years, the village had a mill, two warehouses, and a hotel for boat crews. Its population had tripled.

The canal had a weakness, however. Every December the water froze, and the boats stopped until spring. Merchants stored their goods for months and waited.

In 1858, a railroad line reached Harlow Falls. Like the canal, the railroad lowered the cost of shipping and connected the town to the city. Unlike the canal, it ran all year and covered the trip in a single day. Merchants quickly moved their business to the new rail depot on the east side of town.

The canal did not disappear overnight. Some farmers kept using it because boat rates were lower for very heavy loads. Still, by 1880 the last canal company had closed. The warehouses near the water were turned into a furniture factory, and the old towpath, where mules once walked, later became a walking trail.

Historians sometimes describe the canal and the railroad as two chapters of the same story. Each one solved the problem of distance, and each one reshaped where the people of Harlow Falls lived and worked.$q$, 'draft')
