-- Passage rg8-connections-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0ae7cec1-58ad-59f3-9b55-0ac33ca83430', 'rg8-connections-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Sending a Photo Across the Internet$q$, $q$When you send a photo to a friend's phone, it seems to jump across the city in an instant. In reality, the photo makes its trip in pieces. Computer scientists often explain this process by comparing it to mailing a long story on a stack of postcards.

Imagine you want to mail a story that is too long for one card. You could copy it onto fifty postcards, number each one, and write your friend's address on all of them. Then you drop them into a mailbox. The cards might travel on different trucks and arrive on different days. Because they are numbered, your friend can still put the story back in order.

The internet does something similar. Your phone breaks the photo into thousands of small chunks of data called packets. Each packet carries a label with the destination address and its place in the sequence. Machines called routers read the labels and send each packet along whatever path is least crowded at that moment. Two packets from the same photo may cross completely different routes.

The postcard comparison has limits. A lost postcard is simply gone, and your friend may never know it existed. On the internet, the receiving device notices when a numbered packet is missing and asks for it to be sent again. Also, the whole trip usually takes less than a second, not several days.

Why not send the photo as one big piece? A single large file would block a path while it traveled, much like one enormous truck taking up every lane of a highway. Small packets share the road with millions of other messages. That is why many people can stream, chat, and send pictures on the same network at the same time.$q$, 'draft')
