-- Passage rg8-connections-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('b57e7a0f-cc39-5b75-b2a7-d0379d3d5f79', 'rg8-connections-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Fast and Slow Muscle Fibers$q$, $q$Watch a sprinter explode off the starting blocks, then watch a marathon runner glide past mile twenty. Both athletes are using the same basic parts of the body, yet they are relying on two different types of muscle fiber.

Muscles are made of thousands of long, thin cells called fibers. Scientists sort them into two main categories. Slow-twitch fibers contract with less force, but they can keep working for a long time without tiring. They are packed with tiny blood vessels that deliver oxygen, which gives them a dark red color. Fast-twitch fibers contract quickly and powerfully, but they tire within seconds. They hold fewer blood vessels and look paler.

A helpful way to picture the difference is to think about two kinds of engines. Slow-twitch fibers work like a small, fuel-efficient engine that can run all day on a steady supply. Fast-twitch fibers work like a racing engine that produces a burst of speed but burns through its fuel almost at once.

Every person has both kinds of fibers, usually in a fairly even mix. Some people are born with a few more of one type than the other. Training cannot completely change one category into the other, but it can make each kind of fiber stronger and more efficient.

This explains why coaches design such different workouts. A sprinter like Tomasz Wierzbicki, who trains for the 100 meters, practices short, all-out bursts with long rests in between. A distance runner like Amara Osei spends hours running at a steady pace. Each athlete is training the category of fiber that matters most for the event.

So a sprinter and a marathon runner are not using different bodies. They are drawing on different parts of the same system.$q$, 'draft')
