-- Passage rg8-connections-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5488d23e-a3f0-5e96-809d-7ac41aab5d65', 'rg8-connections-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Two Ways to Paint a Wall$q$, $q$When the town of Riverbend decided to paint murals on two old buildings downtown, it hired two artists with very different working styles. Both murals were finished in the same summer, and both now appear on the town's postcards. The way each artist reached that result, however, could hardly have been more different.

Keiko Tanabe plans everything in advance. Before she lifted a brush, she spent six weeks drawing a detailed sketch of her mural, a scene of the river in autumn. She divided the sketch into a grid of squares, then drew a matching grid on the brick wall in chalk. Square by square, she copied the design at full size. Visitors who watched her said it was like seeing a giant photograph slowly being developed.

Rafael Mendoza works the opposite way. He began his mural with only a rough idea: the history of the bakery across the street. Each morning he talked with people walking by and asked what they remembered about the neighborhood. A retired mail carrier described the old trolley, so Rafael painted a trolley. A group of children asked for the bakery's cat, so the cat appeared on a windowsill. His mural grew more like a conversation than a blueprint.

The two approaches came with different strengths. Keiko's careful planning meant she finished a week early and used exactly the paint she had ordered. Rafael ran out of blue paint twice and had to redo one corner. Yet his mural became the one that neighbors point to most often, because so many of them can find their own memories in it.

Neither artist claims to have found the only correct method. Each one simply works in the way that suits how he or she thinks best.$q$, 'draft')
