-- Passage rg8-connections-p07
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('5b29c154-4450-56e7-8cf3-eb57e6e75982', 'rg8-connections-p07', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$A Hive Works Like a Small City$q$, $q$A honeybee hive can hold more than thirty thousand bees, and every one of them has a job. Biologists sometimes describe a hive as a small city, with its own workers, buildings, and services.

The comparison begins with housing. Bees build their home out of wax, shaping it into thousands of six-sided cells. Some cells serve as nurseries where young bees grow. Others work like storage rooms, holding honey and pollen for the winter. Just as a city has separate areas for homes and warehouses, the hive keeps its nursery near the center and its food stores near the edges.

Like city workers, bees change jobs during their lives, but they do so in a set order. A young bee first cleans cells. A few days later it feeds the growing larvae. Next it builds wax and guards the entrance. Only in the last weeks of its life does it fly outside to gather nectar. In a city, a person might choose almost any job at any age. In a hive, a bee's job depends mostly on how old it is.

The hive also has something like public services. On hot days, groups of bees fan their wings at the entrance to pull cool air through the hive. On cold nights, they cluster together and shiver their muscles to make heat. Bees even have a cleanup crew that carries waste out of the hive.

The city comparison helps readers picture how organized a hive is, but it can mislead in one way. Cities have leaders who make decisions. The queen bee, despite her title, does not give orders. Her main role is to lay eggs. The hive's choices, such as when to find a new home, come from thousands of bees responding to one another.$q$, 'draft')
