-- Passage rg8-connections-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('efb58608-e0b8-546d-8cda-16c7c48eb88b', 'rg8-connections-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g8-connections'), null,
        '6-8', $q$Our School Needs a Repair Cafe$q$, $q$Every June, the trash bins behind Westbrook Middle School fill with things that are only slightly broken. I have seen backpacks with one torn strap, headphones with a loose wire, and a bike with a flat tire. I believe our school should start a monthly repair cafe, where students and volunteers fix these items together instead of throwing them away.

A repair cafe works a lot like our school library. The library does not expect every student to own every book. Instead, it shares books, so one copy serves many readers. A repair cafe would share tools and skills in the same way. One sewing kit and one person who knows how to use it could save dozens of backpacks.

Some people argue that repairs are not worth the time because new items are cheap. That may be true for the price tag, but it ignores a different cost. When a backpack lands in the trash, the cloth, metal, and plastic in it are wasted, along with the energy used to make them. A repair that takes twenty minutes keeps all of that in use.

To keep the cafe organized, items could be sorted into three stations: clothing and bags, electronics, and bikes and sports gear. Each station would have an adult volunteer and a few trained students. The electronics station would need the most supervision, since some repairs involve batteries.

A repair cafe would also teach something that a regular class cannot easily teach. In science class, we read about how circuits work. At the electronics station, a student could actually find the broken wire in a pair of headphones and fix it. That is the difference between knowing about something and knowing how to do it.

Our school already shares books. It is time we shared repairs, too.$q$, 'draft')
